---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import { brokers } from '../data/brokers.js';
import { rankByFee, parseFee, alphabetical } from '../utils/ranking.js';
import { faqSchema, itemListSchema } from '../utils/schema.js';

const path = '/lowest-brokerage-brokers-in-india/';
// The table lists all brokers alphabetically; rankByFee is still used to name
// the cheapest published pricing in the copy and FAQ below.
const listed = alphabetical(brokers);
const delivery = rankByFee(brokers, 'delivery');

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which broker has the lowest brokerage in India?',
    a: `On published headline pricing, ${delivery.ranked[0].name} is the cheapest, advertising zero brokerage across segments - statutory charges still apply. Several brokers (Zerodha, Dhan, Fyers) offer free equity delivery with flat ₹20-or-lower derivatives pricing. Brokers with plan-based pricing cannot be reduced to a single number and are shown as Not Disclosed with links to their official rate cards.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is zero brokerage really free trading?',
    a: 'No. Zero brokerage means the broker charges no commission, but every trade still incurs statutory and exchange charges - STT/CTT, exchange transaction fees, SEBI fees, GST and stamp duty. Zero-brokerage brokers earn revenue from other sources such as float income and value-added services.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do some brokers show Not Disclosed instead of a charge?',
    a: 'Brokers with plan-based or variable pricing (for example bank-backed full-service brokers) do not publish a single flat figure that can be fairly compared with flat-fee brokers, so those cells show Not Disclosed with a link to the official rate card rather than a guessed number.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do lower charges mean a better broker?',
    a: 'Not necessarily. Execution quality, platform stability, margin policies and support all affect real trading outcomes. For small, infrequent delivery investments, however, cost differences compound and are usually the dominant factor.',
  },
];

const schemas = [
  faqSchema(faqs),
  itemListSchema({ name: 'Lowest brokerage brokers in India (published pricing, alphabetical)', path, items: listed }),
];

const fee = (b, f) => b.brokerage?.[f] ?? null;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Lowest Brokerage Brokers in India (2026): Delivery, Intraday & F&O Charges Compared"
  metaTitle="Lowest Brokerage Brokers in India (2026) - Charges Compared"
  description="Published brokerage of Indian brokers, listed alphabetically: delivery, intraday and F&O charges from official pricing pages. Gaps marked Not Disclosed."
  path={path}
  eyebrow="Cost comparison"
  standfirst="Headline brokerage from official pricing pages, listed alphabetically - with plan-based pricing linked to its official rate card instead of squeezed into a fake single number."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Lowest brokerage brokers', path }]}
  schemas={schemas}
>
  <h2>Published brokerage by broker (alphabetical)</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Delivery, intraday and F&amp;O brokerage by broker, alphabetical (official pricing pages)</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">S.No</th><th scope="col">Broker</th><th scope="col">Delivery charge</th><th scope="col">Intraday</th><th scope="col">F&amp;O</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {listed.map((b, i) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="tnum">{i + 1}</td>
            <td><a href={`/brokers/${b.slug}/`}>{b.name}</a></td>
            <td>{fee(b, 'delivery') ?? <span class="nd">Not Disclosed</span>}</td>
            <td>{fee(b, 'intraday') ?? <span class="nd">Not Disclosed</span>}</td>
            <td>{fee(b, 'fno') ?? <span class="nd">Not Disclosed</span>}</td>
            <td><a href={b.pricingUrl} rel="nofollow noopener">Official</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="table-note">
    Brokers are listed alphabetically, not ranked. Cells showing Not Disclosed have plan-based,
    variable or unpublished pricing - see the official rate card. All figures exclude statutory charges.
    Estimate per-order charges with our <a href="/brokerage-calculators/">brokerage calculators</a>,
    or compare any two brokers head-to-head on the <a href="/vs/">comparison hub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the published charges show</h2>
  <p>
    {delivery.ranked[0].name} publishes the lowest headline pricing, with zero brokerage across
    segments. A cluster of flat-fee brokers - {delivery.ranked
      .filter((b) => parseFee(b.brokerage.delivery) === 0 && b.slug !== delivery.ranked[0].slug)
      .map((b) => b.name)
      .join(', ')} - also charge nothing on delivery while capping intraday and F&O at ₹20 or less
    per order. The remaining flat-fee brokers charge up to ₹20 on delivery too. For the
    plan-based brokers, cost depends entirely on the plan you pick and your volumes, which is why
    their cells show Not Disclosed rather than a guessed number.
  </p>

  <h2>Charges beyond brokerage</h2>
  <p>
    Brokerage is only one line on a contract note. Every trade also carries securities transaction
    tax, exchange transaction charges, SEBI turnover fees, GST, and stamp duty; accounts may carry
    AMC, and services like call-and-trade, physical statements or payment gateways can cost extra.
    On a delivery trade at a zero-brokerage broker, statutory charges are effectively your entire
    cost - identical no matter which broker you use.
  </p>

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>
    For pure cost, the published record is unambiguous: zero-brokerage and free-delivery flat-fee
    brokers win, and among them the differences come down to AMC, API pricing and platform
    preference rather than brokerage. Confirm the current schedule on the official page before
    opening an account - pricing changes are frequent and rarely announced loudly.
  </p>

  <Faq faqs={faqs} />

  <SourceList
    sources={brokers.map((b) => ({ label: `${b.name} - official pricing`, url: b.pricingUrl }))}
  />
</ArticleLayout>
